Andrew Kerr <andykerr@SPAMGUARD.blueyonder.co.uk> writes:
> As we know, monsters, including pets, prefer artifact weapons over all 
> others. It would also appear that monsters (or at least pets) prefer 
> any artifact weapon they just picked up over any artifact weapon they 
> are already holding. This is Very Annoying if I want to ensure my most 
> powerful pet gets my most powerful spare artifact, and not some bane I 
> left out for my other pets.
> 
> I've looked through the code, and can't see any place where it may 
> compare an artifact in its inventory with what it is already wielding. 
> This for example, weapon.c function select_hwep()

> 	/* prefer artifacts to everything else */
> 	for(otmp=mtmp->minvent; otmp; otmp = otmp->nobj) {
> 		if (otmp->oclass == WEAPON_CLASS
> 			&& otmp->oartifact && touch_artifact(otmp,mtmp)
> 			&& ((strong && !wearing_shield)
> 			    || !objects[otmp->otyp].oc_bimanual))
> 		    return otmp;
> 	}

Note the for loop there: that looks through the monster's inventory in
order. When a monster picks up an item, it gets inserted at the front
of the minvent chain (add_to_minv(): "/* else insert; don't bother
forcing it to end of chain */"), so newer items are reached (and, if
satisfactory artifacts, selected) first.

> This is sufficiently annoying for me to complain that it is a bug.

It's certainly not a bug; it's working as designed. It may or may not
be a misfeature.

